    "body": "<!--\r\nThanks for your contribution!\r\n-->\r\n\r\nThis PR fixes a heap buffer overflow vulnerability in [set_clear_internal](cci:1://file:///Users/aviralgarg/Everything/cpython/Objects/setobject.c:641:0-690:1) (Issue gh-143546).\r\n\r\n## The Issue\r\nThe function [set_clear_internal](cci:1://file:///Users/aviralgarg/Everything/cpython/Objects/setobject.c:641:0-690:1) could read past the end of the allocated table buffer if the set's `used` count became inconsistent with the actual table state. This inconsistency occurs due to re-entrancy: if [__eq__](cci:1://file:///Users/aviralgarg/Everything/cpython/Lib/test/test_set.py:25:4-26:26) is invoked on a set element during an intersection operation (e.g., [set_iand](cci:1://file:///Users/aviralgarg/Everything/cpython/Objects/setobject.c:1800:0-1817:1)), user code can mutate the set (clearing or resizing it) while the interpreter is still holding pointers to the old table.\r\n\r\n## The Fix\r\nI added a bounds check to the clearing loop in [set_clear_internal](cci:1://file:///Users/aviralgarg/Everything/cpython/Objects/setobject.c:641:0-690:1). The loop condition now strictly enforces `entry < table + oldsize` in addition to checking `used > 0`. This guarantees that the loop terminates safely before accessing invalid memory, even if `so->used` is corrupted or inconsistent with the current table size.\r\n\r\n## Testing\r\nAdded a new regression test [test_reentrant_clear_in_iand](cci:1://file:///Users/aviralgarg/Everything/cpython/Lib/test/test_set.py:679:4-712:20) in [Lib/test/test_set.py](cci:7://file:///Users/aviralgarg/Everything/cpython/Lib/test/test_set.py:0:0-0:0). This test reproduces the crash scenario by defining a custom object with a re-entrant [__eq__](cci:1://file:///Users/aviralgarg/Everything/cpython/Lib/test/test_set.py:25:4-26:26) method that clears the set during a `set_iand` (`&=`) operation.",
